 "alignment": [
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "match_ratio": 0.929,
   "heard": "Storm rolls through, and boom, the whole streak goes dark. Again, last year the average American home sat without power for about 11 hours. Bolts was done waiting."
  },
  {
   "beat": "mission",
   "match_ratio": 0.964,
   "heard": "So he's building a wind turbine taller than his house, 50 feet of steel up on the windy ridge out back. And yes, he pulled the permit first."
  },
  {
   "beat": "how",
   "match_ratio": 1.0,
   "heard": "And here's the deal. Wind pushes three blades, the blades spin a generator, and a tail keeps the whole thing pointed into the breeze. Power runs down a buried cable to a battery bank in the shed."
  },
  {
   "beat": "snag1",
   "match_ratio": 0.923,
   "heard": "first snag, a 50 -foot tower doesn't stand itself up and nobody's climbing that."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hack1",
   "match_ratio": 0.962,
   "heard": "The hack? A tilt -up tower. Build it flat on the ground, then crank it upright with a hand winch. Torque supervises. Torque also steals a washer."
  },
  {
   "beat": "snag2",
   "match_ratio": 0.867,
   "heard": "Second snag, the next storm. When that strong can spin a small turban to pieces."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hack2",
   "match_ratio": 0.966,
   "heard": "So the tail does something sneaky. When the wind gets wild, it folds and the blades swing sideways out of the blast. It's called firling. The turbine basically ducks."
  },
  {
   "beat": "works",
   "match_ratio": 0.98,
   "heard": "And it works! Honest numbers. It's rated one and a half kilowatts, but that's the peak. Small turbines average a small slice of that. On a good windy site, figure around five kilowatt hours a day. That won't run the whole house, but the fridge, the lights, and the phones, covered."
  },
  {
   "beat": "callback",
   "match_ratio": 1.0,
   "heard": "So the next time the street goes dark, one porch light stays on. The neighbor has questions."
  },
  {
   "beat": "teaser",
   "match_ratio": 1.0,
   "heard": "But what happens when the wind stops? Tomorrow, he looks at the creek."
  },
  {
   "beat": "question",
   "match_ratio": 1.0,
   "heard": "Would your HOA let you put this in your yard?"
  }
 ]
